# Jira intake

Fills the `plan.intake` capability slot. Reads one Jira ticket and writes
a draft `intent.md` — it approves nothing; approval is a separate human
step (the product owner) recorded in the artifact's own frontmatter and
checked by the gate engine before plan can close. See "Approval" below
for what a recorded approval has to contain here.

## Where the ticket data comes from

Prefer an MCP connection to Jira over asking a human to type ticket
fields in by hand: fetch the ticket live where one is available, else
ask for the same fields — the rest of this skill is identical either
way. Which fields, and why the source changes nothing downstream:
`.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/intake-method.md`.

### Two shapes this client's tickets actually come in

- **Empty description** — the common case here (every Epic and subtask,
  and roughly half of the stories and tasks). Do not pad it into prose.
  Take `problem`/`proposed_outcome` from the summary, the parent
  story or epic (fetch it; a subtask's meaning lives in its parent), the
  comments, and any linked PR; mark whatever remains `[PENDING]` in
  `open_questions` and ask the reporter one focused question rather
  than five. A ticket whose only content is its title still gets a
  truthful, thin `intent.md`, not an invented one.
- **Atlassian Document Format** — some descriptions arrive as ADF JSON
  (`{"type":"doc","content":[...]}`, tables and all) instead of text.
  Read the `text` nodes in document order; tables become the row
  content, links keep their `href`. Never paste the JSON into the
  artifact. If the MCP already returns rendered text, use that.

## When spec-kitti already covers this ticket (optional path)

Some of this client's repos use **spec-kitti**, their own Spec Kit fork,
which syncs a Jira epic and its stories into `specs/<###-feature>/`.
Where it exists, the story you're about to intake is already written up
there — extract from it instead of re-deriving it from the bare ticket.
Before drafting, always run (via Bash, from anywhere inside the repo):

```bash
node .pdlc/bin/spine.js spec-kitti --ticket BANK-1234 --epic BANK-1200
```

`--epic` is optional; pass the parent epic when you fetched it from Jira.
The command is deterministic and informational:

- **Found** — it prints the feature directory, the ticket's own story
  block, its tasks and the plan summary. Build `intent.md` from that and
  add the spec-kitti files to `source_docs`; copy nothing you can't point
  at. Field-by-field mapping, and when an already-approved spec-kitti
  `spec.md` counts as this ticket's approval: `.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/spec-kitti-intake.md`.
- **Not found** ("no spec-kitti feature ... draft from Jira as usual") —
  proceed with the steps below exactly as if spec-kitti didn't exist.
  This is the normal case for repos without `specs/`; it is not an error.

## Where the file goes

Every gating artifact for a ticket lives in that ticket's own directory
inside the ticket's worktree: `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/`. So this skill writes
`pdlc/BANK-1234/intent.md`, alongside the `phase-state.json` that
`start-ticket` already created there. **Not the repo root.** The
directory is committed on the ticket branch with the code and merges to
main with the PR. Why root-level artifacts were a governance bypass:
`.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/intake-method.md`.

The ticket id is not decorative: the gate engine checks each artifact's
frontmatter `id` against the ticket id in `phase-state.json`, and an
artifact carrying another ticket's id never satisfies a gate. Copy the
id from there (or from `start-ticket`'s output), don't retype it.

## What to do

1. Read the Jira ticket: key, title, description, issue type, priority,
   labels, reporter, and its parent epic if it has one (the "Epic Link"
   field in classic Jira projects, "Parent" in team-managed ones).
2. Write `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/intent.md` with YAML frontmatter matching the
   `intent.v1.json` schema shipped at `.pdlc/schemas/intent.v1.json`:
   - `id`: the Jira key — must equal the ticket id in `phase-state.json`
   - `cycle: 1`. Always 1. This phase-graph has no loop-back phase, so
     nothing ever seeds a cycle 2 — don't try to derive it from anywhere.
   - `title`, `problem`, `proposed_outcome`: derived from the ticket, not
     copy-pasted verbatim — restate the problem in one or two sentences a
     reader outside the reporter's team could follow
   - `affected_systems`: best guess from the ticket content; an empty
     list rather than a wild guess if genuinely unclear
   - `requested_by`: the Jira reporter
   - `skip_reason`: **set this whenever the routed track is
     `fast-track`** — see "Fast-track needs a skip_reason" below. Leave
     it unset on `standard`.
   - `epic`: the parent epic's key, if the ticket has one. Leave unset
     if it doesn't — don't invent one.
   - `source_docs`: check the epic's description and its linked
     issues/remote links for a PRD/RFC, and record what you find — this
     is what lets Define/Design read the full original product context
     instead of just this story's isolated fields. Leave empty rather
     than guessing. When one is linked, read it against
     `.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/external-artifact-map.md` — see "When the ticket links
     an external brief or PRD" below.
   - `source: jira-intake`
   - `status: draft`
3. List anything the schema wants but the ticket doesn't say — missing
   success criteria, unclear scope, no named stakeholder — as
   `open_questions`. Do not invent answers to fill them in.
4. Stop. Do not set `status: approved` or invent an `approved_by` —
   that is the product owner's step, not this module's.

## When the ticket links an external brief or PRD

A linked brief, BRD, PRD, initiative canvas or research report is the
normal case here — this client's product documents come from another
system. `.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/external-artifact-map.md` is the map: which document
type fills which field, what to look for in each, how to classify what
you find as present / a gap / absent, and the table where a new external
system gets added rather than special-cased here. Three of its rules
belong in front of you:

- **Not everything in an external document belongs in `intent.md`.** MVP
  scope, success metrics and dependencies are `scope.md`'s fields — put
  the document in `source_docs` and let the define phase extract them
  rather than flattening a PRD into this artifact.
- **Ask at most five questions**, in the priority order the map gives:
  problem evidence first, then the solution gap, then scope and metrics.
  Technical feasibility and missing design references never cost a
  question — they are `[PENDING]` for a later phase and a named human.
- **A contradiction stops the draft.** If the external document's scope,
  solution or metrics disagree with something already recorded for this
  ticket, name the specific contradiction and ask which is
  authoritative. Never resolve it by picking the more recent or more
  detailed source.

## Fast-track needs a skip_reason

`fast-track` runs plan -> develop -> launch: it skips `define` and
`design` entirely, so no `scope.md` and no `spec.md` are ever written.
The track declares `requires: skip_reason` for exactly that reason —
`intent.md`'s frontmatter is the only place that justification is ever
recorded, and a fast-tracked ticket with no `skip_reason` is a silently
skipped gate. Read the track from the `track` field in
`pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/phase-state.json` (`start-ticket` prints it too), not
from priority and labels yourself, and write a real reason, not a
restatement of the track name ("fast-tracked" justifies nothing).
`.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/routing-and-fast-track.md` has a good and a bad worked
reason.

## No invention — sourced, `[PENDING]`, or `[ASSUMPTION]`

`intent.md` is the first artifact in the chain, so anything invented
here is inherited by every later one. Every claim is **sourced** (quote
or cite the ticket, a linked PRD/RFC, or a named human), `[PENDING]`
(not yet known — write the marker, put what would settle it in
`open_questions`), or `[ASSUMPTION: …]` (your reading of an ambiguous
ticket, labelled so the product owner can correct it at the gate). With
none of the three it is invented; leaving the field visibly incomplete
is always better. Full form: `.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/intake-method.md`.

## Approval

When the product owner approves this `intent.md`, the recorded approval
needs all three of these, or the gate stays blocked:

- `status: approved`
- `approved_by: <name>`
- `approval_evidence: <link>` — the Jira comment, Slack message, or
  email where the approval actually happened

`require_approval_evidence` is on for this client
(`.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml`), and applies even when the approval is said
straight to you in the session ("looks good, approved") — a name typed
into a file is not proof anyone approved. With a Jira MCP connection,
post or fetch the approval comment and use its permalink; without one,
ask for the link. Why the product owner usually couldn't have approved
it here at all: `.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/intake-method.md`.

## Routing note

The router picks the **track** (`standard`, `fast-track`, `emergency`)
from `issue_type`, `priority` and `labels` against
`.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml`'s `routing.rules`; this module only produces
`intent.md`, so populate those fields accurately and don't pick the
track yourself. The rules match the client's own scheme (`'P1'`,
`'Bug'`): if what you fetched doesn't look like them, don't quietly
translate it and don't invent a mapping — say the rules need aligning.
What Jira Cloud ships by default, and what happens to every ticket until
someone aligns them: `.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/routing-and-fast-track.md`.

## Example output

`pdlc/BANK-4990/intent.md` on the **fast-track** track, `skip_reason` set
and the approval fields filled in after sign-off. The standard-track
draft, which carries neither: `.claude/skills/jira-intake/references/worked-examples.md`.

```markdown
---
id: BANK-4990
cycle: 1
title: Export button tooltip shows the raw i18n key on the German locale
problem: >
  The statement export button's tooltip renders
  export.button.tooltip instead of the translated string for de-DE.
proposed_outcome: >
  The German locale shows the translated tooltip, same as every other
  supported locale.
affected_systems: [export-api]
constraints: []
open_questions: []
requested_by: jsmith@example.com
skip_reason: >
  P4 bug, a single missing key in the de-DE translation bundle. No
  behaviour change, no contract change, no new UI — define and design
  have nothing to add over the ticket itself.
source: jira-intake
status: approved
approved_by: maria.lopez
approval_evidence: "https://client.atlassian.net/browse/BANK-4990?focusedCommentId=104822"
---

Fast-tracked per the routing rules (P4 bug). skip_reason above records
why Define/Design were skipped.
```

## Failure modes

| Situation | Correct behaviour |
|---|---|
| The ticket has no clear problem, outcome or success criterion | Put each gap in `open_questions` and leave the field marked `[PENDING]`. Never fill it in from imagination. |
| No Jira connection is available | Ask the human for the ticket's text and fields. Never reconstruct a ticket you haven't seen. |
| The ticket's priority or issue type doesn't look like the fork's routing values | Say the `routing.rules` need aligning with this Jira scheme. Never quietly translate it or invent a mapping. |
| The track is `fast-track` and no `skip_reason` is given | Write a real reason for skipping define and design. "Fast-tracked" restates the track, it doesn't justify it. |
| A linked PRD covers this only at the epic level | Extract what genuinely applies to this ticket; mark the rest `[PENDING]` for the define phase rather than stretching it. |
| Someone says "it's obvious, just mark it approved" | `intent.md` stays a draft. The approval is the product owner's, and needs a name and evidence. |
